<section id="help-books" class="help-topic card">
  <h2>Add your books</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="sources">Sources</a> and press <strong>Link folder…</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose the folder that holds your EPUB and PDF files. Its subfolders are included.</li>
    <li>The folder is scanned straight away and its books appear in the <a href="">Library</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>When you add, move or rename files later, press <strong>Scan</strong> on Sources to bring the library up to date.
    Books whose files are gone are kept and marked as missing, never deleted.</p>
</section>

<section id="help-drive" class="help-topic card">
  <h2>Link Google Drive</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In <a href="settings">Settings</a>, choose how much of your Drive the app may see:
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Only books I pick</strong>: the app sees just the files you choose in Google’s picker.</li>
        <li><strong>Whole Drive, read only</strong>: the app finds every book in the Drive folders you choose.</li>
        <li><strong>Whole Drive, read and write</strong> (the default): as above, and it can also organise and trash
          Drive files.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>On <a href="sources">Sources</a>, press <strong>Link Google Drive</strong>. Your browser opens Google’s
      sign-in.</li>
    <li>Sign in and allow access. If Google says <em>“Google hasn’t verified this app”</em>, press
      <strong>Advanced</strong> and then <strong>Go to readerCompanion</strong>: the app is not yet reviewed by Google,
      and it only ever talks to Google from your computer.</li>
    <li>Back in the app, the Google Drive card shows your account. Press <strong>Add folder…</strong> to choose a
      Drive folder, or <strong>Add books from Drive…</strong> when you picked “Only books I pick”.</li>
    <li>Drive is scanned straight away. Press <strong>Scan Drive</strong> later to pick up new or changed books.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>To change the access level, pick it in Settings, then <strong>Unlink</strong> and link again. Unlinking removes
    the app’s access at Google; your library keeps its books.</p>
</section>

<section id="help-goodreads" class="help-topic card">
  <h2>Import from Goodreads</h2>
  <p>Goodreads lets you download your shelves as a file (a <code>.csv</code> spreadsheet). To get it:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Sign in at <strong>goodreads.com</strong> in a browser (the website, not the phone app).</li>
    <li>Open <strong>My Books</strong>. In the left column, under <strong>Tools</strong>, choose
      <strong>Import and export</strong>.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Export library</strong>. Goodreads prepares the file; this can take a few minutes. Reload the
      page until a link <em>“Your export from …”</em> appears.</li>
    <li>Click the link to download <code>goodreads_library_export.csv</code>.</li>
    <li>In the app, open <a href="sources">Sources</a> and press <strong>Import export file…</strong> on the
      Goodreads card, then choose the downloaded file.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>To update, download a new export the same way and import it again. Nothing you changed in this app is
    overwritten. The app reminds you on the Library page when your last import is getting old.</p>
</section>

<section id="help-text-size" class="help-topic card">
  <h2>Make text larger</h2>
  <p>In <a href="settings">Settings</a>, choose a <strong>Text size</strong>. The whole window grows or shrinks at
    once: menu, pages and covers.</p>
</section>
